        <dc:title>Enhancing Energy Efficiency Through Kubernetes Scheduling Techniques</dc:title>
        <dcterms:abstract>This work explores the utilization of Kubernetes' (HPA) and (VPA) mechanisms to enhance energy efficiency in server infrastructures. The work proposes a merged approach of Kubernetes scheduling techniques to optimize resource allocation based on different workload requirements. The objective is to achieve energy-efficient solutions that improve performance and reduce cost liabilities for organizations. The methodology includes a two-dimensional bin packing problem for long-running services, where pods are allocated with the least number of cluster nodes based on CPU and memory requests. The runtime monitor fields on nodes help group applications with comparable runtime and forecast resource use until the subsequent scaling cycle. Autoscaling techniques, including HPA and VPA, are implemented for long-term processing applications. While VPA modifies the resource requests and limits of containers inside of already-existing pods, HPA modifies the number of replicas based on CPU utilization or custom metrics. The study highlights the difference between HPA and VPA in terms of capacity adjustments. While HPA scales by creating or deleting pods, VPA scales by dynamically altering the CPU or memory in the current pod containers. The work emphasizes synergy between VPA and HPA, where VPA calculates and adjusts the appropriate resource allocation, while HPA scale pods horizontally using custom metrics. The work explores the influence of container and virtual machine (VM) differences on enhancing energy efficiency through Kubernetes' HPA and VPA mechanisms. It delves into how these mechanisms address resource allocation challenges and optimize energy consumption. The paper concludes by highlighting the potential of software-based solutions, specifically Kubernetes, for improving energy efficiency in server infrastructures.</dcterms:abstract>
        <dc:date>2025</dc:date>
        <z:language>eng</z:language>
