        <link:link rdf:resource="#item_297"/>
        <dc:title>The Impact of Directed Pod Eviction on Kubernetes Resource Utilization</dc:title>
        <dcterms:abstract>One of the promises of container orchestration technologies is that they enable effective utilization of hardware resources and thus reduce infrastructure costs. However, as applications scale up and down over time in a Kubernetesmanaged environment, the cluster may enter a state of resource fragmentation. In such a state, the cluster’s hardware cannot be used to its full potential because resources become locked by poor Pod to Node mappings.</dcterms:abstract>
        <dc:date>7/2023</dc:date>
        <dc:identifier>
            <dcterms:URI>
                <rdf:value>https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/document/10254775/</rdf:value>
            </dcterms:URI>
        </dc:identifier>
        <dcterms:dateSubmitted>2026-05-05 09:08:07</dcterms:dateSubmitted>
        <z:language>en</z:language>
        <z:libraryCatalog>DOI.org (Crossref)</z:libraryCatalog>
        <dc:rights>https://doi.org/10.15223/policy-029</dc:rights>
        <bib:pages>81-90</bib:pages>
        <bib:presentedAt>
            <bib:Conference>
                <dc:title>2023 IEEE International Conference on Service-Oriented System Engineering (SOSE)</dc:title>
            </bib:Conference>
        </bib:presentedAt>
    </rdf:Description>
